WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.170
Premises and equipment
must be suitable for

2
00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:04.440
their intended
pharmaceutical manufacturing

3
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.730
operations. Good
Manufacturing Practice expects

4
00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:11.960
facility design, construction,
maintenance and use

5
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:17.560
to minimize errors and contamination
risks. Controls depend on the product,

6
00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.210
process and manufacturing
environment. This

7
00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.880
lesson introduces the
principles rather than

8
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.520
prescribing one universal
room design or classification.

9
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.820
Facility layout should support logical
personnel and material movement and help

10
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:37.020
prevent contamination and mix-ups. Separate
or appropriately controlled flows may be

11
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:43.620
necessary depending on the operation and
risks. Material receipt, storage, dispensing,

12
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:48.660
production and dispatch each require suitable
arrangements. The objective is controlled

13
00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:53.090
movement and traceability, not simply
a visually attractive floor plan.

14
00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:59.130
Cleaning and housekeeping are essential routine
controls. Cleaning procedures should specify

15
00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.910
responsibilities, methods, materials and

16
00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.660
frequencies appropriate
to the area or equipment.

17
00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.550
Where necessary, cleaning
effectiveness must be

18
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.650
demonstrated and
documented. Personnel should

19
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:16.810
promptly report spills, damage or abnormal
conditions according to site procedures.

20
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:21.120
A clean appearance alone is not evidence
that a cleaning process is effective.

21
00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:26.780
Manufacturing equipment should be appropriately
designed, located and maintained for its

22
00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.980
intended use. Product contact surfaces
should be suitable for the materials

23
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.770
and processes involved. Equipment should

24
00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.300
allow effective cleaning
and, where required,

25
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:42.140
sterilization. The level of control depends
on criticality and intended function.

26
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.080
For example, a filling machine
and a general office printer have

27
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.790
different GMP impacts.

28
00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.390
Calibration establishes
the relationship between

29
00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.690
an instrument indication and a reference

30
00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.830
under specified conditions.
Maintenance helps

31
00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:00.070
keep equipment in suitable
operating condition.

32
00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:04.010
Both should be planned, recorded
and assessed for impact when overdue

33
00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:09.670
or unsatisfactory. A calibration
label is a useful visual control but

34
00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:14.150
does not replace review of the applicable
calibration record or equipment status.

35
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.830
Qualification provides
documented evidence that

36
00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:20.690
facilities, utilities and
equipment are suitable

37
00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:25.190
for their intended purpose.
Installation qualification,

38
00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:28.110
operational qualification
and performance qualification

39
00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:32.630
are commonly described as IQ, OQ and PQ.

40
00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:37.650
Their application and scope should follow
a justified lifecycle and risk-based

41
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.850
approach. Acceptance criteria must
be defined for the actual system and

42
00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:47.290
process; there is no universal test
limit for every item of equipment.

43
00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:53.430
Clear equipment identification and status
controls help prevent unintended use. Site

44
00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:58.410
procedures may use status labels, electronic
systems, logbooks and approved records

45
00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.490
to show equipment
availability, maintenance,

46
00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:05.350
calibration, cleaning or
qualification state. Operators

47
00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:10.830
should verify status before use. If
information is missing or inconsistent,

48
00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:14.490
the discrepancy should be escalated
rather than resolved by assumption.

49
00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:20.010
Consider a vessel needed for a scheduled
manufacturing batch. Its calibration

50
00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:24.710
label indicates an expired due date,
while the equipment logbook contains

51
00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:29.230
no recent calibration entry. The
operator should not assume the vessel

52
00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:34.070
is acceptable. The finding should be
reported, and authorized personnel should

53
00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:39.170
assess instrument status, intended use
and any potential product impact under

54
00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.730
site procedures. Work should proceed
only when the equipment is appropriately

55
00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:44.920
released for use.
